ucture the one block\nof size two (relative to the blocks before and after it) if the block is an interior\nblock and only two ways to structure the block of size two (relative to the one\nblock adjacent to it) if it is the \u001crst or last block. Hence a(n, n −2) = 4(n −2).\nLemma 4.1 lists several small cases that can also be handled relatively easily\nand also shows a symmetry found in the b(n, k) case.\nLemma 4.1.\nWe have the following identities:\n1. a(n, n −2) = 4(n −2) for n ≥3.\n2. a(n, 1) = 2(n −2) for n ≥4.\n3. a(n, 2) = 2n(n −3) for n ≥4.\n4. b(n, n −2) = b(n, 2) = 4(n −3) + 2 for n ≥4.\nBased on our data, it also appears the sequences {a(n, k)} and {b(n, k)} may\nhave some other nice features:\nConjecture 4.2. The sequence {a(n, k)}n\nk=0 is log-concave for all n.\nConjecture 4.3. The sequence {a(2n + 1, n)} is enumerated by\nn−1\nX\n\u0013\n2n−i\nfor n ≥1\n\u0013\u0012n −1\ni\ni=0\n(−1)i\n\u00122n −2i\nn −i\nand has generating function\nr\n1 + x\n1 −7x.\nThis formula and generating function appear in OEIS [9] for sequence A085458.\nConjecture 4.4. The set of two-pop-stack sortable permutations of length 2n+1\nwith exactly n ascents has an equal number of permutations with last block of size\none as permutations with last block size greater than one. That is, a(2n+1, n) =\n2b(2n + 1, n) for all n.\n16--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplete Sets (https://ideas.repec.org/a/spr/indpam/v51y2020i3d10.1007_s13226-020-0433-5.html)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Complete Sets ...   * Theophilus Agama ... We also introduce the problem of counting the number of complete subsets of any given set.\n\n# Complete Sets\n\n## Author\n\nListed:\n\n  * Theophilus Agama\n\n(African Institute for Mathematical Science)\n\nRegistered:\n\n  * Theophilus Agama\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper we introduce the concept of completeness of sets. We study this property on the set of integers. We examine how this property is preserved as we carry out various operations compatible with sets. We also introduce the problem of counting the number of complete subsets of any given set. That is, given any interval of integers ℌ ≔ [1, N] and letting $${\\cal C}(N)$$ C ( N ) denotes the complete set counting function, we establish the lower bound $${\\cal C}(N)$$ C ( N ) ≫ N log N.\n\n## Suggested Citation\n\n* Theophilus Agama, 2020. \"Complete Sets,\" Indian Journal of Pure and Applied Mathematics, Springer, vol. 51(3), pages 817-824, September.\nHandle: RePEc:spr:indpam:v:51:y:2020:i:3:d:10.1007_s13226-020-0433-5\nDOI: 10.1007/s13226-020-0433-5\n\n## Download full text from publisher\n\n[Input][Input]File URL: http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s13226-020-0433-5\nFile Function: Abstract\nDownload Restriction: Access to the full text of the articles in this series is restricted.\n\n* * *\n\n## More about this item\n\n### Keywords\n\n[Input][Button: Sets]\n\n;\n\n[Input][Button: completeness]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPointed e-positivity conjecture for unit interval graphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/338456/pointed-e-positivity-conjecture-for-unit-interval-graphs)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; # Pointed e-positivity conjecture for unit interval graphs ... Let $X_{G(m),1}$X G(m),1​ be the pointed chromatic symmetric function with distinguished vertex $1$1. ... Brendan Pawlowski, “Chromatic symmetric functions via the group algebra of S_n”, arXiv:1802.05470 (2024).\n\n# Pointed e-positivity conjecture for unit interval graphs\n\n8 years old · traced to 2018\n\nLet $m=m_1\\cdots m_n$m=m 1​⋯m n​ satisfy $1\\leq m_1\\leq\\cdots\\leq m_n\\leq n$1≤m 1​≤⋯≤m n​≤n and $m_i\\geq i$m i​≥i for each $i$i, and let $G(m)$G(m) be the graph on $[n]$[n] with an edge $(i,j)$(i,j) exactly when $i<j\\leq m_i$i<j≤m i​. Let $X_{G(m),1}$X G(m),1​ be the pointed chromatic symmetric function with distinguished vertex $1$1. A pointed symmetric function is pointed e-positive when it is positive in the pointed elementary-symmetric basis. Unit-interval pointed e-positivity conjecture. $X_{G(m),1}$X G(m),1​ is pointed e-positive. This would provide a pointed analogue of the elementary-positivity phenomenon sought for incomparability graphs of $(\\mathbf{3}+\\mathbf{1})$(3+1)-free posets; the supplied text does not state a proof or resolution.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Brendan Pawlowski, “Chromatic symmetric functions via the group algebra of S_n”, arXiv:1802.05470 (2024).\n\nchromatic-symmetric-functionssymmetric-functionsunit-interval-graphs\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComplete sets (https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.05124)\nciteturn4academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.7 years ago; Authors: Theophilus Agama ... We also introduce the problem of counting the number of complete subsets of any given set.\nTitle: Complete sets\nAuthors: Theophilus Agama\nDate: Mon Feb 12 20:21:56 2018\n\nIn this paper we introduce the concept of completeness of sets. We study this property on the set of integers. We examine how this property is preserved as we carry out various operations compatible with sets. We also introduce the problem of counting the number of complete subsets of any given set. That is, given any interval of integers $\\mathcal{H}:=[1,N]$ and letting $\\mathcal{C}(N)$ denotes the complete set counting function, we establish the lower bound $\\mathcal{C}(N)\\gg N\\log N$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChromatic symmetric functions via the group algebra of S_n - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Publication%3A2700322)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.5 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # Chromatic symmetric functions via the group algebra of S_n ... Schur positivity chromatic symmetric function pointed chromatic symmetric function ...   * A New Approach to the Representation Theory of the Symmetric Groups.\n# Chromatic symmetric functions via the group algebra of S_n\n\nFrom MaRDI portal\n\n(Redirected from Publication:2700322)\nChromatic symmetric functions via the group algebra of \\(S n\\)\n\nJump to:navigation, search\n\nDOI10.5802/ALCO.134MaRDI QIDQ2700322zbMATH OpenOpenAlexFDO\n\nAuthors Brendan Pawlowski\n\nPublication date 20 April 2023\n\nPublished in Algebraic Combinatorics (Search for Journal in Brave)\n\nCopyright license Image Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International\n\nFull work available at URL https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.05470\n\nzbMATH Keywords\n\nSchur positivitychromatic symmetric functionpointed chromatic symmetric function\n\nMathematics Subject Classification ID\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Keough_L in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AKeough_L+in%3Amath)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; @misc{1907.03913, author = {John Engbers and Lauren Keough and Taylor Short}, title = {{I}ndependent {S}ets in n-vertex k-chromatic, \\ell-connected graphs}, year = {2019}, eprint = {1907.03913}, note = {arXiv:1907.03913v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... The first is: what $3$-uniform hypergraph on a ground set of size $n$, having at least $t$ edges, has the most $2$-independent sets? ... We also discuss the problem of maximizing the number of $s$-independent sets in $r$-uniform hypergraphs of fixed size and order, proving some simple results, and conjecture an asymptotically correct general solution to the problem.\n0\n\n@misc{1907.03913, author = {John Engbers and Lauren Keough and Taylor Short}, title = {{I}ndependent {S}ets in n-vertex k-chromatic, \\ell-connected graphs}, year = {2019}, eprint = {1907.03913}, note = {arXiv:1907.03913v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe study the problem of maximizing the number of independent sets in $n$-vertex $k$-chromatic $\\ell$-connected graphs. First we consider maximizing the total number of independent sets in such graphs with $n$ sufficiently large, and for this problem we use a stability argument to find the unique extremal graph. We show that our result holds within the larger family of $n$-vertex $k$-chromatic graphs with minimum degree at least $\\ell$, again for $n$ sufficiently large. We also maximize the number of independent sets of each fixed size in $n$-vertex 3-chromatic 2-connected graphs. We finally address maximizing the number of independent sets of size 2 (equivalently, minimizing the number of edges) over all $n$-vertex $k$-chromatic $\\ell$-connected graphs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvents | Institut des sciences mathématiques (https://ism.uqam.ca/events/)\nciteturn4search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; A close relationship between chromatic symmetric functions and Hessenberg varieties was discovered by John Shareshian and Michelle Wachs, who associated a graph with each regular semisimple Hessenberg variety and formulated a conjecture relating the chromatic symmetric function of the graph with the symmetric function associated with the Hessenberg variety via Tymoczko’s action. ... This picture included substantial evidence that pointed to these groups being all topologically isomorphic to a single group, namely, L^{0}---the non-locally compact, topological group of all Lebesgue measurable functions from [0,1] to the circle. ... We will describe the background touched on above, including the connections with Descriptive Set Theory.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLarissa Kröll - arXiv C